WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:06.038
Actions on objectives describes the point where the adversary attempts to achieve the intended impact.

00:00:06.038 --> 00:00:11.184
The objective may be data theft, fraud, disruption, sabotage, account abuse, ransomware impact,

00:00:11.184 --> 00:00:15.538
business email compromise, operational interference, or something specific to the organization.

00:00:15.538 --> 00:00:19.892
Defenders use this stage to connect technical evidence to business risk.

00:00:19.892 --> 00:00:21.476
What data was touched?

00:00:21.476 --> 00:00:23.059
Which privileged actions occurred?

00:00:23.059 --> 00:00:24.643
Which accounts approved changes?

00:00:24.643 --> 00:00:26.226
Did backups remain usable?

00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:27.414
Were transactions altered?

00:00:27.414 --> 00:00:29.789
Did a business process stop working?

00:00:29.789 --> 00:00:34.143
Monitoring data access, identity activity, privileged actions, payment or workflow changes,

00:00:34.143 --> 00:00:37.310
and unusual export behavior can help identify impact.

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:39.685
Recovery planning matters here as well.

00:00:39.685 --> 00:00:41.664
Backups, continuity procedures, transaction review,

00:00:41.664 --> 00:00:47.602
and communication plans reduce the chance that a late-stage incident becomes a prolonged business crisis.

00:00:47.602 --> 00:00:50.373
This stage should also feed lessons learned.

00:00:50.373 --> 00:00:53.935
If the team only restores service and moves on,

00:00:53.935 --> 00:00:56.706
it misses the chance to strengthen controls.

00:00:56.706 --> 00:01:01.852
Good analysis of actions on objectives explains what outcome was attempted or achieved,

00:01:01.852 --> 00:01:05.415
what evidence supports that conclusion, what business impact remains,

00:01:05.415 --> 00:01:07.790
and what control improvements should follow.

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:10.957
Late-stage analysis should be tied to business language.

00:01:10.957 --> 00:01:14.124
A leader may not need every technical detail,

00:01:14.124 --> 00:01:18.874
but they do need to understand whether data, money, availability, safety, trust,

00:01:18.874 --> 00:01:20.853
or regulatory obligations were affected.

00:01:20.853 --> 00:01:24.020
That translation helps response priorities match organizational risk.
